cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this measurement or its data, please cite it as below."
title: "tokenizer-detect"
abstract: >-
  A prerecorded test of the hypothesis that token fertility explains how well a
  machine text detector works on a language. The X axis is tokens per
  whitespace word on FLORES-200 devtest over 204 languages and 4 tokenizers,
  counted locally with zero model calls. The Y axis is copied verbatim from
  published tables, MULTITuDE macro-F1 and Pangram 4 table 8 FNR and FPR. The
  headline number is the partial Spearman correlation controlling for the share
  of the language in CommonCrawl. The hypothesis is refuted by the condition
  fixed in advance: the sign disagrees between the two sources. The sign of the
  correlation is set by the language the detector was fine-tuned on, so
  fertility stands in for the distance between the training language and the
  language under test. Under a detector fine-tuned on Russian, English ranks
  last of 10 languages while having the lowest fertility of all 10.
